Our top 20 public LB subs had a least of 0.64350 private LB score, which speaks quite good of our validation approach.
Validation Approach
Using
@ogrellier
's idea of Adversarial Validation I used around 15-20 features(mostly time dependent ones) to get the P(test) for each sample. Then I broke the sample's into 10 bins of equal size as Bin 1: 0 < P(test) < 0.1,  Bin 2: 0.1 < P(test) < 0.2, … etc. Each bin was then broken into 2 bins, (One with Positive Class and Other with Negative Class). In total, there were 20 bins and the training and validations sets were stratified using these bins.
One more idea to try could be to create bins of unequal size. We used 5 folds for each of our models and my idea was that both training and validation set for each of the model should have every type of sample(determined by P(test)). Initially when the distribution of data in private and public LB was not known, all folds having the samples of each kind was the safest way to go, and it turned out to be better in the later stages too. We did not spend too much time on time split validation as it was not promising in terms of PLB with our initial tries, returned to it after
@cdeotte
's awesome kernel, but abandoned it again.
Feature Engineering
LGBM:
Our best LGBM model scored 0.695 on the PLB, using only 81 features, almost all of them were original features, encoded in some different way. For almost all of the IdentifierColumns I had used frequency encoding(some log and some non-log) thanks to
@longyin2
's insights, used
@cpmpml
's great and simple code to sort the features with Version in them and split up some of them. Three features which were shown to be of no importance to LightGBM were dropped. There was no removing of unbalanced values in categorical features in LGB,  I wonder if it would have lead to higher private scores.
Note
: Don't  do frequency encoding or feature interactions blindly, try to find which kind of frequency encoding and interactions correlate most with your target. Blind frequency encoding can have an adverse effect on your CVs.
NNs:
Our best NN scored 0.691 on the PLB and 0.669 on the private LB. From the discussions, I found that most of the NNs scored high on the private LB too, which verified the general idea that NNs were overfitting less. We used entity embeddings with 3 layers and 0.691 was achieved in the final week of the competition, it was a long struggle from the initial 0.684. Kagglers getting 0.693/0.697 please do share how did you manage to get so high scores. NNs used a total of 105 features,  these features were created in the last week of the competition too, I tried using them on LGBMs but thanks to Kaggle's memory limit struggles, I was constrained. The main advantage for me using NNs was that each fold took only 30 mins to train so training parallely NNs were much better in terms of speed and accuracy(both considered) than any other model.
FTRLS
FTRLs would not have been possible had it not been for
@tunguz
. Thanks again Bojan for your great Kernel. His great kernel's adaption to our validation scheme of 5 folds helped boost our public LB by 0.001. FTRLs took the same basic features as Bojan's Kernel.
Other Models:
We gave a shot at using Sparse Matrices thanks to
@bogorodvo
, also FTRLs using NNs thanks to
@guoday
Did not experiment very much with these, but they helped in blending/stacking.
Other Ideas
One more idea was regarding misclassifications/good classifications. You can choose to remove either one of them and get 2 new models. This worked quite well in giving us diversity, atleast in the PLB  ;)
Final Submission
We were quite concerned about choosing our last 2 subs and had a lengthy debate. The final idea was to choose an aggressive and a defensive one.
Aggressive
:  was the best PLB of 0.704, which gave us a private LB of 0.64729.
This was a weighted blend of 2 stacks(both at PLB 0.700) and one blend(at PLB 0.702),
this would land us at 48th in the current private LB.
Defensive
was at 0.700 with a correlation of 95% with LGBMs. This was a stack of only NNs and FTRLs going with our idea that NNs and FTRLs were overfitting less. It was a harsh decision,  choosing the other sub so low in PLB,(0.004 difference), but it worked out quite well
I would like to specially mention my team mate
@datahobbit
who worked very hard with stacking inspite of his office hours and gave us NN+ FTRL stack of 0.700 on the last day of the competition which lead to our rank of 25th.
Challenges Faced
The biggest challenge for me and for all those without any powerful machine or resources was memory limitation :D. Also the time it took for each model to run, was huge.
Our issue with stacking was that many of our models with high CVs were not performing equally well in PLB. I admit we went a bit after PLB after seeing sudden increase in the PLBs in
the last 2 weeks. One model at 0.745 would score 0.691, other would 0.694 on the LB, similarly there were many more such disparities. This would lead them to getting equal weightage in the stacking process, which we definitely did not want. We used thresholds and filters to try and get some subset of data which correlated well with the public LB. This was quite risky because the no. of samples were reducing to a great extent and chances of overfitting were increasing. Even this worked only to some extent. The best stacking could get alone was only 0.700. I would love to know if any one you faced similar kind of problems and what did you try?
